title: 'points-converter: limit the units a chain can credit per era and service'
doc:
- audience: Runtime Dev
  description: |-
    `pallet-staking-async-points-converter` now credits at most `UnitsPerEraLimit` units of a
    service per era to each reporting chain. Governance sets the limit per chain and service with
    the new `set_units_per_era_limit` call, from `PointsOrigin`. Without a stored value the new
    config item `DefaultUnitsPerEraLimit` applies, and `MaxUnitsPerEraLimit` bounds what governance
    can set. A limit of zero stops crediting for that chain and service. `CreditedUnits` holds what
    a chain was credited in the active era.

    Each service has its own unit, so `DefaultUnitsPerEraLimit`, `MaxUnitsPerEraLimit` and the
    existing `MaxPointsPerService` give a value per service: they are `Convert<ServiceKind, u32>`.
    `MaxPointsPerService` changes from `Get<u32>`, and the three items are no longer metadata
    constants.

    Each count is first clamped to `MaxBlocksPerEntry`. When a report asks for more than what is
    left of the limit, every entry is cut by the same ratio, rounded down. Units that are not
    credited are dropped and reported in the new `UnitsOverLimit` event. `BlocksCredited` and
    `BlocksIgnored` are renamed to `UnitsCredited` and `UnitsIgnored` and gain a `service` field.
    `UnitsCredited` is emitted only when points reach staking and counts the credited units. A
    report that arrives without an active era now emits `UnitsIgnored`.

    Asset Hub Westend sets `MaxBlocksPerEntry` to the most blocks a system chain can author in
    one expected era at its block processing velocity. For collation, `DefaultUnitsPerEraLimit` is
    twice that and `MaxUnitsPerEraLimit` four times the default.
    No migration is needed: the new storage items are empty on a running chain and their defaults
    apply.
crates:
- name: sp-staking
  bump: major
- name: pallet-staking-async
  bump: minor
- name: pallet-staking-async-points-converter
  bump: major
- name: asset-hub-westend-runtime
  bump: minor
